  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1226 AM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nThis week into next weekend...\n\nToday through late week (Thursday): Morning surface and GOES East \nwater vapor/RAP analysis indicate upper low lifting northeast into W \nVA and VA region of the Appalachians. Continued warmth into mid \nOctober will be the story for the week. Mean ridge around 592-594DM \nwill continue to build in from over the northern Gulf into the Gulf \nCoast states. Dry weather and seasonably warm temperatures (highs in \n90-96F range and lows upper 50s to around 60 degrees in east MS to \nlow to upper 60s in central to northwest Delta) will be the norm. \nHigh temperatures could be nearing records (average is 81). There is \nenough afternoon mixing and drop in afternoon humidity into critical \nlevels (25 to 35 percent range) for a limited fire danger. Kept \nmention of Limited fire danger in HWO graphic. Deep mixing will \nallow for minimum afternoon relative humidity levels to dip (20 to \n30 percent range) most afternoons throughout the week, so limited \nfire danger should persist, especially given light rain totals from \nIsaias. High pressure will keep winds relatively light and should \nlimit greater fire concerns throughout the work week. Thermal ridge \nover the Gulf Coast will usher in low level return flow and heat and \nhumidity. Heat indices will peak in the low to mid 90s but nothing \non the order to support dangerous heat stress we have been dealing \nwith for some time. Ridge will flatten and build to the northeast \ninto mid to late week (Wednesday to Thursday) leading to a more \nsoutheasterly fetch of return flow. Cannot rule out some deep \nmoisture around the periphery of the region (ArkLaTex into Mid \nSouth) could cause a few showers to develop but more likely to \nremain out of the region.\n\nLate week into next weekend (Friday into Sunday): A more fall-like \nfrontal passage looks to finally be on tap into late week into next \nweekend. An upper low will over CA/NV region early week and ejecting \ninto the Four Corners mid week may potentially interact with a \ndeepening trough axis/upper jet diving into central Canada and \nsoutheast into the Great Lakes. Guidance is clustering more towards \nthe earlier solution of synoptic evolution: the Euro retrograding a \nspoke of energy towards the Baja Peninsula while another over the \nLee side of the Rockies. The evolution has trended away from a more \nphased and amplified meridional solution of the GFS earlier \nyesterday. However, a stout, dry cold front will drive dewpoints \nfalling into the mid 30s to mid 40s (36-45F). At the very least, \ncool down remains likely next weekend. Highs will be seasonably warm \nFriday (82-91F), with lows in the low to mid 60s (60-66F) that \nmorning and lows that night upper 40s to low 50s north of Interstate \n20 (48-52F) to mid to upper 50s (53-57F) to the south. Seasonable \nand pleasant fall temperatures will build areawide into next weekend \n(highs of 70-78F Saturday and 76-82F Sunday and lows of 45-50F north \nof Interstate 20 to 50-54F to the south). With continued drought \nstressed vegetation and longstanding limited rainfall, this front \nwill likely increase fire danger concerns. 1035-1045mb surface high \nbuilding will build east out of the lee side of the Rockies through \nthe Mid West, leading to 6-8mb pressure gradient and resultant gusty \ngradient wind/wind gusts reaching 30mph at times. This will be a \nconcern and a fire danger graphic/Elevated risk will likely be \nneeded if these trends persist. /DC/",
